    epilogue: 'Marlowe''s solicitor arrives talking about a misunderstanding between
      partners. Then the file is laid out in order: the master code at 21:38, the
      figure on camera at 21:40, the cup rinsed and wiped clean, the sale he''d known
      about since the 12th.


      He stops talking. Prosecutors charge Elliot Marlowe
      with the murder of Daniel Reyes. He never confesses. He doesn''t have to.'
  - endingId: END_WeakCase
    title: A Thin File
    outcome: 1
    conclusionId: CON_ChargeMarlowe
    conditions:
    - type: 4
      evidence: {fileID: 0}
      fact: {fileID: 0}
      statement: {fileID: 11400000, guid: c23b64e0f27ed104a9dcadc6b602a9bf, type: 2}
      suspect: {fileID: 0}
      id: 
      choice: 
    epilogue: 'Marlowe is charged. He lied about the night and he was there, and
      the file proves that much. But without a motive on record, or anything to show
      what happened in that room, his defence calls it a late visit between old partners
      that ended in an argument.


      The trial is months away, and the case is
      weaker than it should be.'
  - endingId: END_Collapsed
    title: Released by Morning
    outcome: 2
    conclusionId: CON_ChargeMarlowe
    conditions: []
    epilogue: 'Marlowe''s alibi was never broken. His solicitor has him out within
      hours: nothing places him on Harlow Street that night. The charge is dropped
      before breakfast, and Marlowe now knows exactly what you have, and what you
      don''t.'
  - endingId: END_WrongKim
    title: The Wrong Man
    outcome: 2
    conclusionId: CON_ChargeKim
    conditions: []
    epilogue: 'Victor Kim had a grudge and an old key card, and that''s all. The
      access log shows his card was never used that night, and nothing puts him near
      Harlow Street. The charge collapses.


      Whoever was in that office at
      21:40 has had time to get rid of everything that mattered.'
  - endingId: END_WrongHayes
    title: The Wrong Witness
    outcome: 2
    conclusionId: CON_ChargeHayes
    conditions: []
    epilogue: 'Nora Hayes''s card opened the lobby door at 23:47, three minutes before
      she found the body and called it in. The pathologist puts the death between
      21:30 and 22:30.


      The charge doesn''t survive the first hearing, and
      the real killer is still out there.'
  - endingId: END_Unsolved
    title: An Open File
    outcome: 3
    conclusionId: CON_NoCharge
    conditions: []
    epilogue: 'No charge is brought. The file on Daniel Reyes goes back into the
      cabinet in the Evidence Room, still open.


      Somewhere in the city, someone
      knows exactly what happened at 18 Harlow Street, and is starting to believe
      they got away with it.'
  solutionNotes: 'THE TRUTH (writers only). Elliot Marlowe killed Daniel Reyes.

    On
    the 12th Marlowe learned from Calder Holdings'' lawyer that Reyes was selling
    his 60% share, which would put Marlowe''s position ''under review''. On the 14th
    Marlowe left work at 18:40 by key card. At 21:38 he came back and used the master
    code instead of his card, so his card wouldn''t show; this disarmed the alarm
    Reyes had set at 19:10. The lobby camera caught him at 21:40 in a long dark coat,
    carrying the draft agreement in a flat folder. Reyes made them both coffee. They
    argued about the sale; Marlowe struck him with a heavy object from the desk,
    rinsed and wiped his own cup, took the weapon and left by the stairs around 22:15
    (exits by the stairs aren''t logged). He ignored the calls on his phone that
    night.

    Under questioning he admits only the visit and the argument (''He
    was alive when I walked out''), never the killing. Hayes is innocent: she left
    at 17:58 and came back at 23:47 for her laptop, after the time of death. Kim
    is innocent: his card was never used.'
